WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.294
You know, as I'm going through
Greenlights, especially 

00:00:03.294 --> 00:00:05.922
with your father,
"What a complex guy,"  

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:07.090
I thought.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:12.929
Because it seems almost
like violence in some form 

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:14.764
was a love language for him. 

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.934
You tell that story of your 
brother having the rite 

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:20.895
of passage when your father
had taken him to this place

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:22.772
and wanted him to steal
something and your brother

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:24.691
didn't wanna do it, and so
your brother and he get into 

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:27.360
this knockdown drag-out,
and the moment your brother

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.071
hits him with a 2x4 and he's
passed out and your brother's

00:00:30.071 --> 00:00:33.783
like "Oh, Dad, Dad, Dad,"
and then when he comes to...

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.452
"That's my boy."

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:37.037
[laughs]
He thinks that--

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:40.957
"That's my"--crying, weeping
tears, going, "That's my boy. 

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:44.711
Thank you." And they were best
friends from that day on.

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:47.297
Best friends. My dad never
-challenged him morally,

00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:50.550
philosophically, never talked
to him as a younger man,

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:53.011
a son, or a subservient ever
again in his life.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:54.137
And the same thing
happened to you.

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:54.846
They were best friends.

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:57.932
Same thing happened to you in a
rite of passage where you had

00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:01.352
stolen a pizza one night--
not stolen the pizza, but yeah. 

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:02.145
Yeah.

00:01:02.145 --> 00:01:03.146
You had an incident
with a pizza.

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:03.730
I stole it.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:09.819
Okay. You'd had an incident
about a pizza, came home,

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:15.116
and your father asked you about
the pizza, you lied about it, 

00:01:15.116 --> 00:01:18.161
and tell us about that moment.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:24.209
Well, I tried to, you know...
note to children out there:

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:26.002
when you've done something
wrong and you come home

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:28.922
and your parents go, "So, um,
did you pay for that pizza?" 

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.799
If you didn't pay for it, 
it means they know

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:31.883
you didn't, okay?

00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:32.133
Yes.

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:35.178
So I tried to weasel out of it
and go, "Well, I mean, I think.

00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:36.513
I mean, we"... and this--
the more I was groveling,

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:39.557
the more I was digging myself 
a hole, the more my father was 

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:43.228
getting angry and almost feeling
like, "What have I done wrong?

00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:45.980
I'm giving you a chance,
son, to tell me you stole it."

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.149
I think in his mind, he's like,
"I've stole plenty of pizzas

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:50.443
when I was a kid. Just tell 
me." And I didn't have the

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.947
courage to tell him. And soon
as I said, "Well, no, I mean, 

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.740
I thought"-- he gave me a
backhand,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.827
and one that I had earned.
And he sat there and he's like, 

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:01.454
"What am I supposed to do, son? 
What am I supposed to do?

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.748
Just tell me. Be man enough to
just tell me." And if I

00:02:03.748 --> 00:02:07.418
would've told him, "Hey, Dad,
yeah, we stole a pizza.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:09.254
Geez, I didn't know we'd ge
caught," he'd have been like,

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:11.589
"Gosh damnit. Now, son,
what'd I teach you?

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:13.800
You shouldn't do that. And then,
damnit, if you're gonna do it, 

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.926
get away with it better."

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:16.094
[laughs]

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:18.346
But I got my rite of passage
later in life with him.

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:22.725
Yes, that night over the bar,
having-- yeah, punching out

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:27.313
a guy in the moment and...
Who had put his hand on my dad.

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:27.856
Yes.

00:02:27.856 --> 00:02:28.273
Yeah.

00:02:28.273 --> 00:02:31.943
And coming out of it, 
your dad said, "That's my boy," 

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:33.027
in so many words. 

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:34.571
He basically pulled me off the
guy and said, 

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:37.824
"That's enough, son. That's
enough." And then I remember 

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:39.742
him calling his friends and,
you know, saying,

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:42.120
"Hey, the youngest one"
--is what he called me.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.164
The youngest one. Matthew.
I was the mama's boy-- "

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:47.125
"he's gonna be okay.
He's gonna be okay.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:48.168
I mean, he's got

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.503
a bit of a berserker switch,
but he's gonna be okay." 

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:54.132
And that was, you know...
for him, yeah,

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.634
it was initiations and rites of 
passage. And, you know, he grew

00:02:56.634 --> 00:02:58.845
up a different generation.
He was a physical man, as you

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:02.098
read in the book. Those hands
that could hurt were also those

00:03:02.098 --> 00:03:07.270
hands that healed, from birds
to my mother to his children, 

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:08.354
you know?

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:11.608
Well, you just mentioned being
a mama's boy, a self-proclaimed

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:14.110
mama's boy. You write about
spending most of your time with 

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.449
your mother growing up because
your dad was often on the road, 

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:20.825
and how do you think 
that shaped you?

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:25.705
Hmm. Well, Mama and I were good 
buddies. I mean, she was my

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:28.082
kindergarten teacher, she was a 
seventh grade teacher, she was 

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:30.210
my substitute teacher along the 
way, we would ride to school

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.296
together on a bike and we'd and
we'd mimic that Froot Loop

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:36.090
commercial with Toucan Sam. 
"Right behind you, baby. 

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:41.054
Right behind you, baby."  And 
Dad wasn't gone for long

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:43.890
periods of time.  He would 
just be on the road travelling

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.185
through Texas peddling pipe, 
selling pipe, trying to get

00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:51.356
accounts. He was not my little 
league coach or my soccer coach

00:03:51.356 --> 00:03:53.691
or football coach like he was 
maybe for my older brothers,

00:03:53.691 --> 00:03:57.654
but I understood. I mean, we 
had moved there... Longview was 

00:03:57.654 --> 00:04:00.323
a fast-growing place, the oil 
business was good, he was busy,

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:05.203
and so I spent a lot of time 
with Mom. It wasn't like Dad

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:07.705
wasn't in my life. I just didn't
have him in my daily life like

00:04:07.705 --> 00:04:09.332
maybe my big brothers did.

